Release Notes for RPMC Tool


Version:	1.6.1.0
Release Date:	August 12, 2021

About RPMC Tool:

     RPMC is designed to set RPMC address. 

Important Notes:

·	To set RPMC address is a dangerous operation, CPU might get useless permanently and cannot be recovered. Please do NOT run this tool if you don’t know how to use it.
Configuration Requirements:
CPU:
·	AMD Ryzen 4000 series

BIOS:
·	Please make sure the BIOS includes the PSP firmware with RPMC support.
Motherboard:
·	Please make sure the motherboard has RPMC SPI ROM.

Operating Systems:
·	RHEL 7.5
Note: RPMC tool was validated with these Operating Systems. Other Operating Systems may work, but aren’t supported since they have not been validated. RPMC tool is a built against x64, any 32 bit OS is not supported.
Installation Notes:
·	Just unzip the RPMC_xx.tar.gz file.
RPMC Usage:
Note: Please run RPMC tool with administrator privileges, otherwise the driver cannot be loaded correctly. 

rpmctool.exe <function> <option> 
Functions:
-- help                    Show tool version and usage 
-- check                  Check RPMC is enabled or not 
-- bes                      Check if each bit is enabled for “--check -n” command 
-- set [RPMC_ADDR] (-r)     Set RPMC to RPMC_ADDR, -r to force restart
Options
-n                        Return a number of string
Meaning of each bit when set:
BIT0: Command succeeded
BIT1: RPMC is enabled
BIT2: SPI-ROM: RPMC counter address 0 is available to set
BIT3: SPI-ROM: RPMC counter address 1 is available to set
BIT4: SPI-ROM: RPMC counter address 2 is available to set
BIT5: SPI-ROM: RPMC counter address 3 is available to set
BIT6: PSP/fTPM: RPMC counter address 0 is in use
BIT7: PSP/fTPM: RPMC counter address 1 is in use
BIT8: PSP/fTPM: RPMC counter address 2 is in use
BIT9: PSP/fTPM: RPMC counter address 3 is in use
BIT10: Fuse bit was set for RPMC Root Key Provision done at Manufacturing (Production Key)
BIT11: RPMC SPIROM is available
BIT12*: ASIC: RPMC slot 0 is available to use
BIT13*: ASIC: RPMC slot 1 is available to use
BIT14*: ASIC: RPMC slot 2 is available to use
BIT15*: ASIC: RPMC slot 3 is available to use
BIT16*: ASIC: RPMC slot 4 is available to use
BIT17*: ASIC: RPMC slot 5 is available to use
BIT18*: ASIC: RPMC slot 6 is available to use
BIT19*: ASIC: RPMC slot is full
BIT20*: ASIC: RPMC slot availability version bit 0
BIT21*: ASIC: RPMC slot availability version bit 1


*: Currently we have two method to get RPMC slot availability. If BIT21 BIT20 = 0x0, then BIT12~BIT15 means if slot 0~3 available. If BIT21 BIT20 = 0x1, then if BIT19 is set, then all slots are used; else, calculate BIT18~BIT12 = X, slot 0 ~ (X-1) is used, and slot X ~ slot 15 is available.





